// Chapter 1 — Documents, fields and blocks.
//
// The exemplar chapter (#159). Every snippet below was run against the
// `wcl` CLI before it was written down; nothing here is paraphrased from
// the older concept units without being checked first.
page lang_documents {
  title = "Documents, fields and blocks"

  h1 "Documents, fields and blocks"

  p "A `.wcl` file is a **document**. It is not a stream of settings — it is a tree of _items_, and the tree has a shape that a schema describes and the tool checks. This chapter covers the three items you write most (fields, blocks and tables), the two that navigate the tree (`self` and `parent`), the one that deliberately leaves no trace (`let`), and the ten more that the rest of the book expands on."

  p "Everything here runs. Type the files, run the commands, compare the output."

  h2 "A file is a document"

  p "Start with a service inventory. Save it as `inventory.wcl`:"

  code wcl {
    filename = "inventory.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
# inventory.wcl — every service we run, and where.

# What a service is.
@block("service")
type Service {
  @inline(0) name: identifier
  port:   u32
  region: utf8
  @child("limits") limits: Limits?
}

# What a service may spend.
@block("limits")
type Limits {
  cpu:    f64
  memory: std.ByteSize
}

# What the file as a whole may contain.
@document
type Inventory {
  owner: utf8
  @children("service") services: list<Service>
}

owner = "platform"

service web {
  port   = 8080u32
  region = "us-east-1"

  limits {
    cpu    = 0.5
    memory = 512MiB
  }
}

service db {
  port   = 5432u32
  region = "us-east-1"
}
WCL
  }

  p "Three commands read it. `wcl check` validates the document against its schema and says nothing else:"

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ wcl check inventory.wcl
OK
TEXT
  }

  p "`wcl get` resolves a dotted path and prints one value:"

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ wcl get inventory.wcl owner
"platform"
$ wcl get inventory.wcl services.web.port
8080u32
$ wcl get inventory.wcl services.web.limits.memory
536870912
TEXT
  }

  p "`wcl parse` prints the whole evaluated tree, so you can see what the document became rather than what you typed:"

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ wcl parse inventory.wcl
...
owner = "platform"
service web {
  port = 8080u32
  region = "us-east-1"
  limits {
    cpu = 0.5
    memory = 536870912
  }
}
service db {
  port = 5432u32
  region = "us-east-1"
}
TEXT
  }

  p "Read the path `services.web.limits.memory` from the left. `services` is the field the `@document` schema declares to gather every `service` block. `web` picks one of them by its label. `limits` is the nested block. `memory` is a field on it, and `512MiB` has already become the number 536870912 — units are part of the value, not part of the text. [Values and primitives](lang_values) covers that; [Schemas](lang_schemas) covers `@document`, `@block`, `@child` and `@children`."

  callout "The schema is optional, but the checking is not" {
    class = ["note"]
    body  = "A file with no `@document` type still parses and still evaluates. What it loses is `wcl check`: with nothing to check against, an unknown field is just another field. Every example in this chapter carries its schema so the error messages are real."
  }

  h2 "The thirteen item forms"

  p "A document is a list of items, and there are exactly thirteen kinds of item. The list below is the map of the whole language — most chapters of this book are one row of it in depth."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Item"                   | "Written as"                              | "Covered in" |
      | "Field"                  | "`name = expr`"                           | "[This chapter](#fields)" |
      | "Block"                  | "`kind \"label\" { … }`"                    | "[This chapter](#blocks-and-labels)" |
      | "Table"                  | "`name:` then one pipe row per record"    | "[This chapter](#tables)" |
      | "`let` item"             | "`let name = expr`"                       | "[This chapter](#let-items)" |
      | "`type` declaration"     | "`type Name { … }`"                       | "[Types](lang_types), [Schemas](lang_schemas)" |
      | "`interface` declaration"| "`interface Name { … }`"                  | "[Types](lang_types)" |
      | "`union` declaration"    | "`union Name { … }`"                      | "[Types](lang_types)" |
      | "`symbol_set` declaration" | "`symbol_set Name { … }`"               | "[Values and primitives](lang_values)" |
      | "`namespace` declaration"| "`namespace app.web`"                     | "[Namespaces and imports](lang_namespaces)" |
      | "`use` declaration"      | "`use lib.Tier`"                          | "[Namespaces and imports](lang_namespaces)" |
      | "`import`"               | "`import \"./lib.wcl\"` or `import <wdoc.wcl>`" | "[Namespaces and imports](lang_namespaces)" |
      | "`connection` declaration" | "`connection DependsOn: Service -> Service : EdgeKind`" | "[Connections](lang_connections)" |
      | "Connection statement"   | "`web -> db :uses`"                       | "[Connections](lang_connections)" |
  }

  p "Thirteen forms, fourteen spellings: `fn name(a: T) -> U { … }` is a **`let` item** in disguise. It binds a name to a function value exactly as `let name = fn(a: T) -> U { … }` does, and differs only in that the editor tooling indexes it. See [Functions](lang_functions)."

  p "Every one of the fourteen is legal in one file. This one parses and checks:"

  code wcl {
    filename = "map.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
namespace app                      # 1. namespace declaration
import "./lib.wcl"                 # 2. import
use lib.Tier                       # 3. use declaration

symbol_set EdgeKind { uses }       # 4. symbol_set declaration

interface Named {                  # 5. interface declaration
  name: utf8
}

union Scale {                      # 6. union declaration
  Small { replicas: u32 }
  Large { replicas: u32  shards: u32 }
}

@table("route")
type Route {                       # 7. type declaration
  verb: utf8
  path: utf8
}

@block("service")
type Service {
  @inline(0) name: utf8
  port:  u32
  scale: Scale
  @children("route") routes: list<Route>
}

connection DependsOn: Service -> Service : EdgeKind   # 8. connection declaration

@document
type Cfg {
  owner: utf8
  @children("service")    services: list<Service>
  @connections(DependsOn) deps:     list<DependsOn>
}

let team = "platform"              # 9. let item

fn label(s: utf8) -> utf8 { s }    # 10. fn item — a let item in disguise

owner = label(team)                # 11. field

service "web" {                    # 12. block
  port  = 8080u32
  scale = Scale::Small { replicas: 2u32 }
  routes:                          # 13. table
    | "GET"  | "/users" |
    | "POST" | "/users" |
}

service "db" { port = 5432u32  scale = Scale::Large { replicas: 5u32, shards: 3u32 } }

web -> db :uses                    # 14. connection statement
WCL
  }

  p "Order matters in one place only: `import` must come before the `use` that names something the import brought in. Otherwise items may appear in any order — a field may read a `let` declared below it, and a block may use a type declared at the end of the file."

  h2 "Comments"

  p "There are two line-comment forms, `//` and `#`. Both run to the end of the line. There are **no block comments**: `/* … */` is a parse error."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
// A leading comment describes the next item.
service "web" {
  port   = 8080u32   // a trailing comment sits on the same line
  # hash comments work too
  region = "us-east-1"
}
WCL
  }

  p "Comments are part of the tree, not stripped noise. `wcl fmt` and the `wcl set` edit path both keep them, and they keep the blank lines that group your items — up to one blank line between any two items. What `wcl fmt` does not keep is the choice of marker: it rewrites `//` to `#`."

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ cat two-forms.wcl
// The edge service.
# Two comment forms, one meaning.
@block("service")
type Service {
  @inline(0) name: utf8
  port: u32   // the listening port
}

$ wcl fmt two-forms.wcl
# The edge service.
# Two comment forms, one meaning.
@block("service")
type Service {
  @inline(0) name: utf8
  port: u32  # the listening port
}
TEXT
  }

  h3 "Doc comments"

  p "A **doc comment** is not a third form. It is the unbroken run of line comments directly above a declaration. \"Unbroken\" is the rule that matters: a blank line ends the run, and anything above the blank line belongs to nothing."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
// The edge service: terminates TLS and forwards to the app tier.
// Owned by the platform team.
@block("service")
type Service {
  @inline(0) name: utf8
  port: u32
}
WCL
  }

  p "The `doc_comment` builtin reads it back, which is how the language server fills a hover card and how a generated reference page gets its prose:"

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ wcl get service.wcl service_doc
"The edge service: terminates TLS and forwards to the app tier.\nOwned by the platform team."
TEXT
  }

  p "Doc comments attach to declarations — types, interfaces, unions, union variants, symbol sets and the fields inside them. A comment above a _field_ or a _block instance_ is an ordinary comment: kept, formatted, and not readable as data. [Builtins](lang_builtins) documents `doc_comment`."

  h2 "Fields"

  p "A field binds a name to a value with `=`:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
name    = "alpha"
count   = 3u32
enabled = true
ratio   = count / 2u32
WCL
  }

  p "The right-hand side is any expression, not just a literal: a reference to another name, a call, arithmetic, a conditional. [Expressions and operators](lang_expressions) covers the whole grammar. Fields evaluate **lazily** — nothing on the right of an `=` runs until something asks for that field, which is why `ratio` may name `count` regardless of which line came first. [How a document evaluates](lang_evaluation) covers the order, the caching and the cycle detection."

  p "Fields are the leaves of the document. Every other item exists to group them, constrain them, or compute them."

  h2 "Blocks and labels"

  p "A block is a named group of fields that may also hold other blocks. The name before the brace is the block's **kind**; the schema for that kind decides which fields are legal inside it."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
service "web" {
  port   = 8080u32
  region = "us-east-1"
}
WCL
  }

  p "Between the kind and the brace you may write **labels** — positional values that identify the instance. A label is any scalar: a string, a bare identifier, a number, a boolean or a symbol. The schema binds each position to a field name with `@inline(n)`:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
@block("route")
type Route {
  @inline(0) verb: utf8      # first label
  @inline(1) path: utf8      # second label
  handler: identifier
}

# Two labels: verb = "GET", path = "/users".
route "GET" "/users" {
  handler = list_users
}
WCL
  }

  p "A block whose body is empty may drop the braces entirely, which makes a label-only instance read like a declaration:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
flag beta { on = true }
flag legacy
WCL
  }

  p "A kind may also be namespace-qualified — `wdoc::process { … }` names the `process` kind in the `wdoc` namespace, and `foo.bar::process` a deeper one. See [Namespaces and imports](lang_namespaces)."

  callout "A label is not a readable field" {
    class = ["warning"]
    body  = "`@inline(n)` tells the schema which field a label _fills_, and the document model reads labels through the block's label list. It does not make the label resolvable by that name from an expression: inside the block above, `self.verb` does not evaluate to `\"GET\"`. When a sibling field has to read the value, declare it as an ordinary field and write it out."
  }

  h2 "Nesting"

  p "A block may hold further blocks, to any depth. What is legal at each level is the schema's business, not the parser's: `@child(\"kind\")` admits one, `@children(\"kind\")` admits a list."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
service "web" {
  metadata {
    region = "us-east-1"
    tags {
      environment = "prod"
    }
  }
}
WCL
  }

  p "Nesting is how a dotted path gets long. `wcl get inventory.wcl services.web.limits.memory` walks the gather field, then the label, then the nested block, then the field."

  p "A block that the enclosing schema does not admit is an error, and so is a field the block's own schema does not declare. Add `zone = \"a\"` to the `web` service of `inventory.wcl` and `wcl check` names the field, the schema and nothing else:"

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ wcl check inventory.wcl
wcl::eval::schema_violation

  × field 'zone' is not declared by schema 'Service'

inventory.wcl: 1 schema violation
TEXT
  }

  h2 "Tables"

  p "When many blocks of one kind share a shape and carry nothing but scalars, writing each as a block is noise. A **table** writes the same data one record per line. Give the row type a `@table(\"kind\")` decorator instead of `@block(\"kind\")`, then write the parent's field name with a colon and the rows underneath:"

  code wcl {
    filename = "api.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
@table("route")
type Route {
  verb: utf8
  path: utf8
  auth: bool
}

@block("api")
type Api {
  @inline(0) name: utf8
  @children("route") routes: list<Route>
}

@document
type Cfg {
  @children("api") apis: list<Api>
}

api "public" {
  routes:
    | "GET"    | "/users"     | false |
    | "POST"   | "/users"     | true  |
    | "DELETE" | "/users/:id" | true  |
}
WCL
  }

  p "Note the two spellings of one name. The **header** is the parent field (`routes:`, declared as `@children(\"route\")`), and each row becomes one `route`. The colon is what distinguishes a table header from a field: `routes = …` is a field, `routes:` opens a table."

  p "Three rules follow from a row being a block:"

  list {
    li "**Cells are positional.** They fill the row type's fields in declaration order — first cell to the first declared field, and so on. Nothing names a column."
    li "**A row must be complete.** Supply exactly one cell per declared field. A short or long row is a schema violation: `table row for 'route' has 2 values, expected 3`."
    li "**A cell is an expression, evaluated as a literal.** Strings, numbers, booleans and symbols all work. A bare identifier does not — write it as a string."
  }

  p "The trailing pipe is optional, and a header with no rows at all is legal. Tables live **inside a block**; a table written at the document's top level parses, but its rows are not projected into a root gather field."

  p "One thing a table is not is addressable. `wcl get` walks fields and labelled blocks, and a row has neither a name nor a label, so no dotted path reaches into one. Rows are for the program that consumes the document — a `wdoc` build, a host reading the document through the Rust API — not for the command line."

  h2 "self and parent"

  p "Two contextual keywords navigate the tree from inside it. `self` is the block the expression is written in. `parent` is the block one level out."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
service web {
  port       = 8080u32
  region     = "us-east-1"
  admin_port = self.port + 1u32     # this block's own `port`

  limits {
    # `parent` reaches back out to the service.
    label = $"limits for ${parent.region}"
  }
}
WCL
  }

  p "Both are keywords only in expression position, so a field or type named `self` or `parent` keeps working everywhere else. At the top level of a file `self` is the document itself, and `parent` is an error — there is no scope above the root. One level in, `parent` is the document."

  callout "Navigators, not copies" {
    class = ["note"]
    body  = "`self` and `parent` resolve lazily through the lexical scope chain, exactly as a `&T` reference field does. They read the value at the moment the field is forced; they do not snapshot it when the block is parsed."
  }

  h2 "let items"

  p "A `let` item binds a name to a value the way a field does:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
let base_port = 8000u32

service "web" {
  port       = base_port
  admin_port = self.port + 1u32
}
WCL
  }

  p "The difference is that a `let` is **not document data**. It is a composition helper: sibling and descendant expressions resolve it by name, and nothing else can see it at all."

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ wcl get scratch.wcl services.web.port
8000u32
$ wcl get scratch.wcl base_port
no such path: base_port
TEXT
  }

  p "That invisibility is deliberate and it is total. A `let` does not appear in the document's field list or block list, `wcl get` cannot address it, it is absent from `wcl parse` output and from a JSON serialization of the document, and the schema never validates it. The last one is the useful one:"

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ cat field.wcl        # ... zone = "a" inside `service "web"`
$ wcl check field.wcl
wcl::eval::schema_violation

  × field 'zone' is not declared by schema 'Service'

field.wcl: 1 schema violation

$ cat let.wcl          # ... let zone = "a" inside `service "web"`
$ wcl check let.wcl
OK
TEXT
  }

  p "Use a `let` for the intermediate value that exists so the real fields stay readable — a base port, a shared prefix, a computed list you map over twice. Use a field for anything a consumer of the document is meant to read."

  callout "Two lets, one keyword" {
    class = ["info"]
    body  = "The `let` **item** described here sits at file or block scope and takes no semicolon. There is also an expression-level `let name = expr;` that lives inside a `{ … }` block expression and is scoped to it. They share a keyword and nothing else. See [Expressions and operators](lang_expressions)."
  }

  h2 "Four ways to write a value"

  p "Fields, blocks, tables and `let` items all put a value in a file, and they are not interchangeable. The table below is the comparison — what each form is _for_, and what the document model can see of it."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Form"     | "Holds"                        | "Reachable by `wcl get`"              | "Schema-checked" | "Reach for it when" |
      | "Field"    | "One value"                    | "Yes"                                 | "Yes"            | "A consumer reads this value" |
      | "Block"    | "A group of fields and blocks" | "Yes — by kind, or by gather field and label" | "Yes"    | "The value is a thing with parts" |
      | "Table"    | "Many blocks of one kind"      | "No — a host walks the rows"          | "Yes, per row"   | "Many records share one flat shape" |
      | "`let`"    | "One value"                    | "No"                                  | "No"             | "Only other expressions read it" |
  }

  p "Run the difference rather than trusting the table. Take the inventory from the top of this chapter and add `zone = \"a\"` to the `web` service: `wcl check` refuses it, because `zone` is not on the `Service` schema. Change that one line to `let zone = \"a\"` and the same command prints `OK` — the schema never saw it. Then ask for the value: `wcl get inventory.wcl services.web.zone` answers `no such path`. The binding did its work during evaluation and left nothing behind."

  p "That is the whole idea of a WCL document in one experiment. What you write is either data — visible, addressable, checked against a declared shape — or it is scaffolding that helps you write the data, and the document keeps the two apart for you."

  h2 "Where to go next"

  list {
    li "[Values and primitives](lang_values) — what may sit on the right of an `=`: the numeric types, strings and heredocs, symbols, units."
    li "[Lists, tensors and records](lang_collections) — the composite values behind a `list<T>` gather field."
    li "[Schemas](lang_schemas) — `@document`, `@block`, `@child`, `@children`, `@inline`, `@table`, and what `wcl check` actually checks."
    li "[How a document evaluates](lang_evaluation) — laziness, caching, cycles, and the order things really run in."
    li "[The CLI](lang_cli) — `check`, `parse`, `get`, `set`, `fmt`, `diff` and the rest in full."
  }
}
